      "text": "To determine how many positive integers less than 1992 are not factorial tails, we need to understand the concept of trailing zeros in factorials and how to count them.\n\nFirst, let's calculate how trailing zeroes are formed. For any integer \\( n \\), the number of trailing zeroes in \\( n! \\) is determined by the number of times \\( n! \\) can be divided by 10. Since \\( 10 = 2 \\times 5 \\), and in \\( n! \\), there are usually more multiples of 2 than multiples of 5, the number of trailing zeroes is determined by the number of times \\( n! \\) can be divided by 5.\n\nThe formula to find the number of trailing zeroes in \\( n! \\) is:\n\\[ z(n) = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots \\]\n\nWe need to find all numbers less than 1992 that cannot be formed by some \\( m! \\). This means we need to include in our count all numbers of the form \\( m! \\) minus the count of trailing zeroes until we cover all numbers less than 1992.\n\nIf we calculate all possible numbers of trailing zeroes up to 1991 (since trailing zeroes in \\( n! \\) must be lower than 1992), we can recover a count of all numbers. Since numbers whose decimal representation ends in \\( n \\) zeroes are \\( 10^n, 10^n + 1, 10^n + 2, \\ldots, 10^n + (1991 - n) \\), we can determine a range for the counter, and for each range, check what subset of these we have to add to those with trailing zero counts for them to reach 1992.\n\nNext, we plan to evaluate conditions that'll permit us to iterate through, logically isolate correct values and systematically review each value to verify its membership in the original set, save it for later recall, and form a sorted set of results.\n\nUse negative indexing to check for each statement:\n```python\nvalid_numbers = set(range(1992)).difference(range(1992))\n \t\n# Generate factorials which are less than 1992\nfact_values = set()\nfor i in range(1, 1992//5 + 3):\n\tfact_values.add(i *\"5\" + str(i))\n\n# Here we eliminate all multiples of any factorials from valid_numbers\nit = valid_numbers - fact_values\n\n# Since we sorted it before, it should shift back to consecutive values reasonably\nvalid_numbers_list = list(it)\nprint((len(valid_numbers_list), valid_numbers_list))\n```",
